<edge source="global_currency_friction::fx_intervention_level" target="taiwan_cross_strait::technology_transfer_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Frequent or large official FX interventions are often accompanied by tighter FX monitoring and controls (capital‑flow management) that directly complicate cross‑border payments, royalties and joint‑venture funding, prompting firms to scale back or slow formal technology licensing and co‑development agreements with Taiwanese partners.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_currency_friction</data>
  <data key="d40">taiwan_cross_strait</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_currency_friction::fx_intervention_level" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::trade_control_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Large or highly visible official FX interventions (e.g., persistent currency defense or managed depreciation) are perceived as competitive economic measures and tend to trigger reciprocal political and regulatory responses, including tighter export controls and licensing on sensitive technologies.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_currency_friction</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
