<edge source="global_currency_friction::exchange_rate_level" target="hormuz_standoff::maritime_incident_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A materially stronger reserve currency (e.g., USD) increases the real purchasing power of US/allied defence and security budgets for forward deployments and escorts, sustaining patrol presence in the Strait and lowering the frequency of hostile or interdiction incidents.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_currency_friction</data>
  <data key="d40">hormuz_standoff</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_currency_friction::exchange_rate_level" target="russia_ukraine_war::component_availability" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">A depreciation of the ruble (a rise in the ruble‑per‑USD exchange rate) raises the local currency price of imported drone parts, reducing procurement volumes and diversity of foreign suppliers for components.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_currency_friction</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_currency_friction::exchange_rate_level" target="taiwan_cross_strait::manufacturing_export_flo" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A stronger foreign currency (e.g., USD appreciation / higher USD/TWD exchange rate) makes Taiwan's USD‑priced manufactured goods cheaper for buyers abroad, directly raising export volumes as exporters win or expand orders and pass through price competitiveness.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_currency_friction</data>
  <data key="d40">taiwan_cross_strait</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_currency_friction::exchange_rate_level" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::critical_materials_avail" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Sharp adverse moves in bilateral exchange rates (an importer’s currency weakening) raise the local‑currency cost of imported critical minerals and precursors, causing buyers to curtail orders and suppliers to reallocate stock, reducing near‑term availability for high‑tech manufacturing.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_currency_friction</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
